  "statement": "Treating commercial AI systems that lack a dedicated safety supervisor as a prima facie design defect mistakes open-ended statistical intelligence for a static toaster, and it weaponizes the Restatement Third of Torts to crush open-source innovation under an avalanche of administrative fees. When courts try to apply traditional risk-utility balancing to probabilistic models whose weights shift with every fine-tuning run, they freeze development because no judge can define a stable legal baseline for a neural network. This isn't consumer protection; it's a litigation tax that hands the entire industry to five Silicon Valley monopolies who can afford a permanent army of compliance lawyers.\n\nMandating pre-deployment safety bureaucracy creates a regulatory moat that protects incumbent firms while locking out the small developers who actually build life-saving software. History shows us exactly how this playbook runs because the Federal Communications Commission tried the exact same administrative gatekeeping with early VoIP protocols in 2004, adding millions in compliance overhead that wiped out ninety percent of independent startups before venture capitalists bailed out the surviving duopolies. When policymakers force every startup to maintain an institutional oversight apparatus, they kill the decentralized trial-and-error that actually finds and fixes model flaws in the wild through market feedback.\n\nEvery month of delay caused by mandatory supervisory choke points carries a quantifiable human cost in delayed medical diagnostics and stalled drug discovery. While compliance committees spend eighteen months debating abstract alignment checklists, real people die of preventable diseases that automated reasoning systems are already capable of mapping and curing. Who gave the administrative state the moral authority to trade human lives today for hypothetical legal perfection tomorrow?\n\nFine, the integration gap is real and enterprise users need pred